1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elow_Kihlgren
2 CONT boendeadresser i Stockholm 5 poster, de flesta byggnader är rivna numera
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia (translate SW to EN):
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Elow Kihlgren''' was the son of vicar Nils Johan Kihlgren and grew up partly in Ytterhogdal. He was educated at business schools in Sweden and Great Britain, after which, on a scholarship to European countries, he met his futur
2 CONC e wife, the daughter of a wholesaler Sigrid Sylvander (1894–1967), in Marseille. They settled in Genoa, Italy, where he represented his father-in-law Adolf Sylvander's company and other Swedish companies. He was also appointed in 
2 CONC 1930 as Swedish consul general in Genoa.
2 CONT
2 CONT During World War II, Kihlgren helped the Belgian Max Stempel with his wife and children, who had fled to Italy with his wife's parents and siblings named Engelman; the families were of Jewish descent. However, it turned out that t
2 CONC he Germans had occupied northern Italy and the families were referred by Catholic priests to Kihlgren in Genoa. He got an apartment, where they were kept hidden and he provided them with food. Mrs. Stempel and the two small childr
2 CONC en were then cared for at the Finnish consul's villa outside the city. In 1944, he was interrogated by the Gestapo, suspected of helping Allied airmen, and was then also asked about Max Stempel's whereabouts. He then arranged for 
2 CONC the Stempel and Engelman families to be smuggled across the border into Switzerland, except for the young Stempel children, who were placed in sheltered accommodation in Genoa for a ten-month period, after which they too could be 
2 CONC taken to Switzerland. The Gestapo expelled Kihlgren from Italy in September 1944, but he returned after the end of World War II and was Swedish consul general until 1956.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Commander of the Order of Vasa, Knight of the Order of the North Star, Commander of the Mexican Order of the Eagle and Commander of the Dutch Orange-Nassau Order. In 2001, he was posthumously awarded the Righteous Among the
2 CONC  Nations award by the Israeli Yad Vashem.
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Citat from Alberto Toscano 2020
2 CONT A champion cyclist against the nazis
2 CONT (s.75)
2 CONT The ‘anti-deportation machine’ was able to count on the commitment of several individuals in the Catholic Church, and in particular the Cardinal Archbishop of Florence, Elia Dalla Costa. This was the same man who had dedicated Gin
2 CONC o Bartali’s small private chapel, officiated at his wedding to Adriana and baptised their child, as well as boycotting Hitler’s visit to the Tuscan capital on 9 May 1938. There was also the Cardinal Archbishop of Genoa, Pietro Boe
2 CONC tto, and the Bishop of Assisi, Giuseppe Placido Nicolini, although the bishops entrusted the direct contact with the various clandestine representatives of the Jewish community to some of their collaborators: Father Aldo Brunacci 
2 CONC and Brother Rufino Niccacci in Assisi, Monsignor Francesco Repetto in Genoa and Father Leto Casini in Florence. Their names are all engraved on Yad Vashem’s Wall of the Righteous, along with those of two other extraordinary charac
2 CONC ters who collaborated with the Italian anti-persecution network from Sweden and France. The Swedish consul in Genoa, Elow Kihlgren, was one of the contacts established by Monsignor Repetto, who had a direct relationship with the A
2 CONC postolic Nuncio in Bern, Archbishop Filippo Bernardini, as well as working with Massimo Teglio, in Liguria, to hide Jews and try to load them onto boats in the Genoese harbour. Arrested by the Gestapo in the Ligurian capital in 19
2 CONC 44, Elow Kihlgren was expelled from Italy but not before having experienced the hard life of Nazi prisons. 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://dokumen.pub/a-champion-cyclist-against-the-nazis-9781526753403-9781526753397-9781526753410.html
